    }
}
```
The solution uses bit manipulation to check if `n` is a power of three. We start by initializing a `long` variable `x` to a large value (1000000007 in this case) and a `long` variable `y` to 3. We then enter a while loop that continues as long as `y` is less than or equal to `n`. In each iteration of the loop, we check if `n` is divisible by `y` by performing the modulo operation `n % y`. If `n` is divisible by `y`, we return `true` since `n` is a power of three. Otherwise, we multiply `y` by 3 and continue to the next iteration of the loop. If we reach a point where `y` is greater than `n`, we return `false` since `n` is not a power of three.

